`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["In the eager forward, the matrix multiplications and the attention kernel take only ",o(h.eagerHeavyShare)," of the kernel time. The rest goes to normalization, bf16 casts, the rotary embedding, copies, residual additions and the activation: kernels that perform no model FLOPs and are limited by memory bandwidth. Compilation fuses them and cuts their time from ",R(h.eagerRest)," to ",R(h.compiledRest),". Kernels keep the GPU busy almost the whole forward in both modes, so most of the time compilation saves sits in these kernels, not in the gaps between them."]}),`
`,a.jsx(e.li,{children:"Normalization is the largest of these costs. The residual stream stays in fp32, because the embedding table is fp32 and autocast runs only the matrix multiplications and attention in bf16. So RMSNorm reads and writes fp32, and each Linear that reads a norm's output casts it to bf16 on its own. The compiled forward fuses each residual addition, normalization and cast into one kernel."}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["The MLP's matrix multiplications run at the card's ceiling. Counting only the matrix multiplications and the attention kernel, the eager forward would reach about ",o(h.heavyOnlyMfu)," MFU. With the rest of its time it reaches ",o(h.eagerMfu,1),". Compiled, the attention kernel, the MLP matrix multiplications and the output head each run ",o(h.compiledSlowdown.min)," to ",o(h.compiledSlowdown.max)," slower: both modes hold the card at its power limit, and the compiled forward, with few memory-bound kernels between its matrix multiplications, reaches the limit at a ",o(h.clockDrop)," lower clock."]}),`
